# Line endings.
#
# The development machine is Windows and `core.autocrlf` is true, so without
# this file every text file is checked out with CRLF. For most of the repository
# that is harmless. For the files below it is not:
#
#   *.sh          a shell script with CRLF fails on Linux with the famously
#                 unhelpful "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M". These scripts only
#                 ever run on the server, so they are never read by Windows.
#   Dockerfile    read inside a Linux build; CRLF has bitten heredocs and
#                 line continuations before.
#   *.yaml/*.yml  compose and Litestream configuration, parsed on the server.
#   *.env*        read by Docker Compose and by Litestream, on Linux.
#
# These are declared explicitly rather than with a repository-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf`, which would rewrite every tracked file at once and bury
# the next real change in a renormalisation diff.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.env.example  text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf

# Go sources are already LF in the index; say so, so they stay that way.
*.go        text eol=lf

# SQL migrations are embedded in the binary and applied by goose on Linux.
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.png       binary
*.db        binary
